# Two-Axis Code Review: L3 Tools Layer and L4 Desktop Environment Hosts
File: openOODA/audits/nextgen/axis/l3_l4_findings.oot

Provenance: Batch 8 two-axis review. Part 1 of 2 (see `l3_l4_evidence.oot` for the raw evidence).

Scope: 8 canonical `openOODA-DE` repositories and 8 consolidated `openOODA-tools` hosts, all reviewed
at `origin/master`: `oocomp oopanel ooportal ooterm oofiles oosettings oobazaar ooagent-hud` and
`oocap oorepl oosemanticdiff oocsplit oobinview oohexdump oostrdiff oosqlite`.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic placeholders.

Axes applied: **Standards** (the shape law of rules.oot 1.8, the single-owner persona law of
`persona-ownership.oot`, the layer-honest I/O rule of RFC 0012, and the Fowler code smells) and
**Spec** (the `anchor.oo` contract of each repository, the `oohat cycle` quorum, `lint-oo-size`, the
negative-trust refusal of the exit code 2, and `make build` / `make verify` / `make test`).

Review method: the tools hosts were reviewed from clean clones of `origin/master`, because the live
trees carry uncommitted work of a concurrent session. Every change was made in the clone, verified
there, and pushed from there.

## 3. Defects found and fixed

Table columns: ID | Defect | Where | Repair

- **D1** | The shape law of rules.oot 1.8 was broken across the pages of the host: several lines
  carried more than one statement, two lines ran past the 128 column bound, the main entry point
  carried 84 lines of code against the bound of 64 (`LONGFN`), and the JSON field parser nested
  eight blocks deep against the bound of four (`DEEP`). The gate answered with `quorum_mask=104`,
  missing the white and the yellow hats, with 8 to 9 standards findings.
  | `openOODA-tools/oocap` | Every page is reshaped into one statement per line. The entry point
  gives its six actions to helpers of their own, and the field parser gives the value search to two
  helpers. The token stream of every page is unchanged, so the tool answers exactly as before.
  Committed `f1f742b`.

- **D2** | The shape law was broken across the pages of the host, and the tool answered every
  unknown option with the exit code 0 and the banner of the default mode, so a mistyped switch
  looked like a successful run.
  | `openOODA-tools/oorepl` | The pages are reshaped into one statement per line, and the CLI now
  names the switches it answers and refuses the first option it does not answer, with the exit
  code 2 and the name of the offending option. Committed `f1915c5`.

- **D3** | The shape law was broken across the pages of the host, and the tool answered every
  unknown option with the exit code 0 and the banner of the default mode.
  | `openOODA-tools/oosemanticdiff` | The pages are reshaped into one statement per line, and the
  CLI refuses the first option it does not answer, with the exit code 2. Committed `59cac6d`.

- **D4** | The option parser already found the options the tool does not know, but the run answered
  them with the exit code 1, the code of a failed read, while a usage error is the exit code 2 of
  the fleet contract.
  | `openOODA-tools/oocsplit` | The run answers an unknown option with the exit code 2 and keeps
  the message that names the option. Committed `2d0a43b`.

- **D5** | The host answered a `--persona` flag pair and picked the persona from its value.
  Rule 1.25 of rules.oot bans the persona flags of the consolidated tools, and RFC 0012 section 2.2
  (amendment `702649a`) bans the flag family fleet-wide. The repository's own Makefile asserted the
  banned behaviour with four test targets.
  | `openOODA-tools/oosqlite` | The flag pair and the code that read it are gone. The host now
  refuses the first option it does not answer, with the exit code 2, and the gate tests hold the
  refusal of the persona flags and of an unknown option. Committed `a950381`.

- **D6** | The host answered the `--personas` option with the table of its personas, the same flag
  family that Rule 1.25 bans. The byte comparison engine also swallowed every option it did not
  know without a word.
  | `openOODA-tools/oostrdiff` | The table and the option are gone. The host refuses the first
  option it does not answer, with the exit code 2, and the byte comparison engine refuses the
  options it does not answer the same way. Committed `be41a07`.

- **D7** | The tool was a wrapper. Its POSIX surface handed the arguments to the host `od` and
  printed what the host printed, so the tool did no work of its own and it failed under the empty
  path, where the host binary is not found. The parity gate did not see it either: it compared the
  tool with the upstream tool, and the tool was the upstream tool. The native engine was also lossy
  at the byte level: it read the file as a string and stopped at the first byte that is not valid
  text, so a file with a high byte lost everything behind it.
  | `openOODA-tools/oohexdump` | The engine is native now. It reads the file under the capability
  token and renders the dump from the bytes of the file, and it answers the eight formats of the
  `od` surface, the four address radixes, the read window, the width and the duplicate marker. The
  reading is at the byte level, so a file of any bytes is dumped whole. The parity gate grew
  eleven match cases that hold the engine against the upstream tool byte for byte. Committed
  `1d0aa90`.

- **D8** | The test of the component name held the name of the host before the rename and failed on
  `origin/master`: `23/24 assertions`. The repository could not pass `make test` before this batch.
  | `openOODA-tools/oostrdiff` | The assertion holds the name of the host now. Fixed in the same
  commit as D6, `be41a07`.

## 4. Findings reported, not repaired

These are ecosystem-wide policy, ownership, or tooling questions. Each one crosses more than one
repository or one layer, so fixing it inside a single repo would create a divergent fork of the
fleet. They are recorded here for the maintainers.

- **F1** | The gate does not catch a shell-out to the tool a host replaces. The red hat counts a
  host binary leak when the source holds a `sys_exec` call and one of ` dd `, ` tar `, ` gzip `,
  ` openssl ` or ` sha256sum `, or when the source holds a hardcoded path. A call to ` od `, the
  very tool `oohexdump` replaces, is not on that list, so the host reported
  `host_bin_leaks=0` while it was a wrapper. The same hat reports `path_nonexistent_ok=1` after
  checking only that the `PATH` variable is set and holds no newline; it never runs the binary
  under an empty path.
  | `openOODA-agents/oohat` | Not repaired here: the gate belongs to another repository and the
  finding list is its own. The defect was found by running the binary under `PATH=/nonexistent`
  and comparing the output with the upstream tool, which is the check the hat claims to make.

- **F2** | The language has no lowering for the error stream. `eprintln` is a known name but the
  compiler refuses a call that carries a string, and the runtime exposes only a newline to the
  error stream. The tools that must write to the error stream shell out to `sh -c printf`, which is
  the last host binary call left in the production paths of the tools layer: `oocap/ipc/diag.oo`
  and `oostrdiff/persona/bindiff/main.oo`. The diagnostic of `oohexdump` therefore goes to the
  standard output, while the upstream tool writes it to the error stream.
  | `openOODA-core/oodac`, `openOODA-core/oodar` | Not repaired here: the language is the holder of
  the capability. The deviation of `oohexdump` is declared in its own `qa/parity/deviations.oot`.

- **F3** | The tools layer is the only layer that answers the retired repository names as the
  subcommands of its hosts: `oostrdiff astdiff`, `oostrdiff bindiff`, `oosqlite ooquery`,
  `oosqlite sqlite`. The apps and DE layers accept the retired names too and the cloud layer
  refuses them, so the reading of the clause of Rule 1.25 that bans the retired names is not the
  same across the fleet.
  | `openOODA/openOODA` | Not repaired here. The persona table of `persona-ownership.oot` lists
  the tokens `ooastdiff`, `oobindiff` and `ooquery` as the owned personas of `oostrdiff` and
  `oosqlite`, so the ownership is recorded and the reading of the clause is a policy question.

- **F4** | The ownership table does not carry six of the eight hosts of this batch. The L3 table of
  `persona-ownership.oot` names `oostrdiff` and `oosqlite`, but `oocap`, `oorepl`,
  `oosemanticdiff`, `oocsplit`, `oobinview` and `oohexdump` are absent, so their persona tokens are
  unrecorded.
  | `openOODA/openOODA` | Not repaired here: the ownership table is the holder of the ownership.

- **F5** | The catalog index still carries the entries of the hosts before the rename, and they
  name products that no longer exist: `oobiew`, `oocsplt`, `oostructureddiff` and `ooquery`. Six of
  the eight hosts of this batch have no entry at all: `oorepl`, `oocsplit`, `oobinview`,
  `oohexdump`, `oostrdiff` and `oosqlite`.
  | `openOODA/catalog` | Not repaired here: the catalog is the holder of the catalogue.

- **F6** | The gate of `oosqlite` creates a symbolic link inside its own build cache to test the
  dispatch on the name of the binary, and the rule of the zero symlinks directive bans the symbolic
  links anywhere in the repository trees. The link is created and removed inside the test, so no
  artefact is shipped, but the pattern is written in a Makefile of the fleet.
  | `openOODA-tools/oosqlite` | Not repaired here. The test needs the dispatch on the name of the
  binary, and the mechanism has no other way to be tested without a link.

- **F7** | The findings of the earlier batches are still open: the contradiction of RFC 0012
  between section 2.2 and section 4.5 on `--list-personas`, the `--mode` selector of the DE layer,
  the release workflow that is absent in the DE layer, the `ooflash` release that is missing, the
  `E_PERSONA_DUP` of the twelve absorbed repositories of the DE layer, the nine of the seventeen
  applications that ship a standalone `ooda.pkg` while the hosts `oooffice` and `oohostmon` do not
  exist, and the thirty-seven repositories over the bound of thirty-two pages of rules.oot 1.8.
  | `openOODA/openOODA` | Not repaired here: each one crosses more than one repository.

## 5. Result

All 16 repositories of this batch answer with `quorum_mask=255`, zero standards findings and zero
spec findings. The eight tools hosts answer with `lint-oo-size` exit 0, with `make build`,
`make verify` and `make test` at exit 0 and `ALL TESTS PASSED`, with the shape law free of
violations, with every page passing `oodac check`, and with the exit code 2 for an unknown option,
for `--persona`, for `--persona=` and for `--list-personas`. The eight DE hosts were re-verified at
`quorum_mask=255` and needed no change.
